//? Title: Min-conflicts n-queens
//? Subtitle: Actual repair choices, verified terminal boards and honest caps
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Place one queen in each row of a square board so that no two share a column or
diagonal. Local repairs can search this constraint space without enumerating all
assignments. We inspect real column costs and random tie choices, retain the
original eight-queen board and size sweep, and distinguish a verified solution
from a capped unsuccessful run. Repair counts alone do not establish runtime
independence from board size.
)

\toc

> Local costs and a global pair count

With one queen per row, queens attack when their columns match or the absolute
column difference equals their row difference. Occupancy arrays count columns
and both diagonals. After removing the selected queen, its cost at column c is
the sum of these three occupancies. Moving from old cost to a minimum cost
changes the total unordered attacking-pair count by the new cost minus the old
cost. This count cannot increase on a minimum-cost repair because the original
column remains an available candidate.

The algorithm initializes rows greedily at random tied minimum-cost columns. A
repair first selects one currently conflicting row, removes that queen, scans
every column and randomly chooses a tied minimum. It permits sideways moves and
can stagnate; no restart or completeness guarantee is implied. Random choices
use an actual MT19937-64 word modulo candidate count, preserving legacy
scheduling and its small modulo bias.

> Observe one real choice on a four-row board

The next program shows the last greedy initialization decision for four queens
and every actual repair. Its first diagram reports the observed row, complete
candidate-cost list and actual selected column. These are producer records
rather than costs recomputed by report code. The table distinguishes
initialization from repair and reports actual attacking-pair counts.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/queens_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::min_conflicts_nqueens_trace(4, 30, 7);
    auto &m = t.moves[3];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("row", {0, 3.75},
           std::format("Actual initial row {}\npairs before {}", m.row,
                       m.conflicts_before));
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < m.costs.size(); ++c) {
        auto id = std::to_string(c);
        d.node(id, {7, double(c) * 2.5},
               std::format("column {} / actual cost {}{}", c, m.costs[c],
                           int(c) == m.column_after ? " / chosen" : "")).edge(
            "row", id);
    }
    std::print("| phase | row | old column | new column | pairs before | pairs after |\n| --- | ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.moves)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                   r.initialization ? "initialize" : "repair", r.row,
                   r.column_before, r.column_after, r.conflicts_before,
                   r.conflicts_after);
    std::print("\nTerminal solved: {}. Actual repairs: {}.\n", t.result.solved,
               t.result.steps);
    d.save_svg("build/queens_tiny.svg");
}
```
