> Retain the original eight-queen board

The original call uses n=8, repair cap1000 and seed42. The table and point plot
show the returned assignment with its terminal solved flag. Row0 is drawn at the
top; x is column plus one-half and y is7 minus row plus one-half. Equal numeric
limits and equal drawing extents preserve square-board geometry. The ordinary
function explicitly verifies the final board even when the last permitted repair
finds a solution.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/queens_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = dm::algorithms::min_conflicts_nqueens(8, 1000, 42);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("Actual solved: {}. Repairs: {}.\n\n| row | actual column |\n| ---: | ---: |\n",
               r.solved, r.steps);
    for (std::size_t row = 0; row < r.queens.size(); ++row) {
        x.push_back(r.queens[row] + .5);
        y.push_back(7 - row + .5);
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", row, r.queens[row]);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual queens", {37, 99, 235}, 9).size(850, 850).margins(
        95, 35, 60, 70).x_limits(0, 8).y_limits(0, 8).title(
        "Original eight queens: actual final board").x_label("column + 0.5").y_label("7 - row + 0.5").save_svg("build/queens_original.svg");
}
```
