> Retain the original size sweep

The original sizes are8,20,50,100,250,500,1000,2000, with seed7+n and repair
cap50n. Every returned solved flag is printed alongside its repair count. The
figure shows repairs, not elapsed time. Initialization scans n columns for each
of n rows, costing O(n squared); each repair scans rows and columns, costing
O(n). Thus O(n squared+n times cap) work and O(n) driver storage describe this
implementation, even if the observed repair counts happen to grow slowly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/queens_scaling.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| board size | seed | cap | actual repairs | verified solved |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "| --- |\n");
    for (int n : {8, 20, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000, 2000}) {
        auto r = dm::algorithms::min_conflicts_nqueens(n, 50 * n, 7U + n);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(r.steps);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, 7 + n, 50 * n, r.steps,
                   r.solved);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual repair moves", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Original size sweep: repairs, not elapsed time").x_label("board size n").y_label("actual repair moves").save_svg("build/queens_scaling.svg");
}
```
