> Plateaus, impossible sizes and capture limits

Two or three queens have no solution under these constraints. A run can exhaust
its cap with equal or decreasing attacking-pair counts without reaching zero.
The three separate panels below contrast their actual plateaus with four queens
under the same cap, preserving identical axis limits so overlapping trajectories
remain visible. Even for a solvable size, an unsuccessful capped heuristic run
does not prove unsatisfiability. Zero repairs still performs greedy
initialization and checks its terminal assignment. Size zero returns a solved
empty assignment; negative size or cap throws. The implementation guards
occupancy arithmetic before allocation.

A trace owns all decisions and records actual random words, complete costs, tied
columns and conflicting rows. Its cap counts three scalar inputs, the final n
columns, and each move record plus its stored row IDs, costs and candidate IDs.
Default1000000, zero disables. Quota failure throws without returning a partial
trace. Capturing total-pair diagnostics adds O(n) work per move, within the
ordinary asymptotic bounds; it changes neither RNG scheduling nor choices.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/queens_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(3, 1);
    std::print("| n | cap | repairs | solved | captured units |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {2, 3, 4}) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::min_conflicts_nqueens_trace(n, 30, 7);
        std::vector<double> x, y;
        for (auto &m : t.moves)
            if (!m.initialization) {
                x.push_back(x.size() + 1);
        y.push_back(m.conflicts_after);
            }
            std::print("| {} | 30 | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, t.result.steps,
                       t.result.solved, t.captured_entries);
            auto colour =
                n == 2 ? dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38} : n == 3
                ? dm::graphics::RGB{22, 163, 74}
                : dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235};
            auto panel = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
            if (!x.empty())
                panel.points(x, y, std::format("n={}", n), colour, 5);
            panel.title(std::format("n={}: actual repair trajectory", n)).x_limits(0, 31).y_limits(-.1, 1.5).x_label("repair number").y_label("attacking pairs");
            layout.add(panel);
    }
    layout.fit_panels().save_svg("build/queens_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | cap | repairs | solved | captured units |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 2 | 30 | 30 | false | 225 |
| 3 | 30 | 30 | false | 250 |
| 4 | 30 | 22 | true | 220 |
\image(figures/queens_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual pair-count trajectories for two, three and four queens.)
\alttext(Unsolvable sizes can plateau; a successful run must reach zero and pass terminal verification.)

> Conclusion and references

The real candidate costs explain each greedy repair and the nonincreasing pair
count. The original board and size sweep retain their exact inputs and honest
status flags. These finite runs demonstrate heuristic behavior, while the
explicit quadratic initialization rules out a size-independent runtime claim for
this implementation.

[Minton and colleagues, Minimizing conflicts: a heuristic repair method for constraint satisfaction and scheduling problems (1992)|https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(92)90007-K]
describes the repair approach. The greedy-random initialization, seeded choices
and capture contract here describe the native implementation.

